 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Current-Level Lerp
-A method that performs interpolation on the leaf cell (current level) rather than the coarsest level, making the interpolant adaptive [S1, unofficial]. It calculates the logical coordinate index $lX' = \min(D[0'].l, D[X'].l)$ and checks if $lX' == \text{oct}.l$ [S1, unofficial]. If true, it averages the inputs; otherwise, it finds the leaf cell level using `findLeafCell(oct[X'].p).l` and interpolates on that level: $\text{oct}[X'].v = \text{lerpOnLevel}(\text{level}, \text{oct}[X'].p)$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from Coarsest-Level Lerp by adapting to the finest available resolution at the sample point [S1, unofficial]. Not given in the sources.
+A method that performs interpolation on the leaf cell (current level) rather than the coarsest level, making the interpolant adaptive [S1, unofficial]. It calculates the logical coordinate index $lX' = \min(D[0'].l, D[X'].l)$ and checks if $lX' == \text{oct}.l$ [S1, unofficial]. If true, it averages the inputs; otherwise, it finds the leaf cell level using `findLeafCell(oct[X'].p).l` and interpolates on that level: $\text{oct}[X'].v = \text{lerpOnLevel}(\text{level}, \text{oct}[X'].p)$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from Coarsest-Level Lerp by adapting to the finest available resolution at the sample point [S1, unofficial]. Not given in the sources. [S1, unofficial]
 
 ### Finest-Level Interpolation
-Trilinear interpolation performed on a fixed logical level $l$ [S2, unofficial]. It views the level as a structured grid where non-existing cells are defined via the Closest Existing Cell (CEC) operator [S2, unofficial]. It is cheap and continuous but not adaptive [S2, unofficial]. Unlike Coarsest-Level Lerp, which dynamically selects the coarsest level involved in the boundary, Finest-Level Interpolation uses a pre-selected fixed level [S2, unofficial]. Not given in the sources.
